"""Renders RESULTS_A.md from results_a.json so every number in the report is regenerated by one command."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
R = Path(__file__).parent
r = json.loads((R / "results_a.json").read_text())
a, c, b, p, ch = r["a_hand_rules"], r["c_compiled_rules"], r["b_llm_query_time"], r["parser"], r["change"]
ran_b = "status" not in b
NR = "NOT RUN"
bv = lambda k, f=lambda x: x: f(b[k]) if ran_b and k in b else NR
md = f"""# RESULTS_A - Income-tax engine: rules vs LLM vs LLM-compiled rules

> Generated by `python -m project_a.evaluate && python -m project_a.report_a`. Suite: **{r['n_cases']} cases**, expected values
> from `tests/oracle.py` (independent representation, same author) + 12 hand-computed golden cases (`tests/test_golden.py`).
> **Not done in the build environment (no internet):** the 30-case check against the official ITD calculator
> (`tests/official_check_template.csv` is pre-filled with our numbers - fill the two `official_*` columns) and any Gemini run.
> Cells marked NOT RUN are never simulated. Re-run with `GEMINI_API_KEY` set to fill them.

## 1. Comparison table (Task 4)
| Metric | (a) Hand-coded rules | (b) LLM at query time | (c) LLM-compiled rules |
|---|---|---|---|
| Rupee-exact accuracy (both regimes) | {a['exact_pct']}% ({a['n']} cases) | {bv('exact_pct', lambda x: f"{{x}}% on {{b['n_subset']}}-case subset")} | {c['exact_pct']}% ({c['n']} cases) |
| Deterministic over 10 repeats | {a['deterministic_pct_10x']}% of 60 inputs | {bv('determinism_pct_10x_on_5_inputs', lambda x: f"{{x}}% of 5 inputs")} | {c['deterministic_pct_10x']}% of 60 inputs |
| Latency p50 / p95 | {a['latency_ms_p50']} / {a['latency_ms_p95']} ms | {bv('latency_s_p50', lambda x: f"{{x:.1f}} s p50")} | {c['latency_ms_p50']} / {c['latency_ms_p95']} ms |
| Cost per 1,000 requests | ${a['cpu_usd_per_1000']} (CPU, assumed ${r['assumptions']['cpu_usd_per_hr']}/vCPU-h) | {bv('usd_per_1000')}; est. {b.get('est_in_tokens_per_request', b.get('avg_in_tokens', '?'))} in-tokens/request | ${c['cpu_usd_per_1000']} (CPU) |
| Every rupee traces to a clause | {a['traceable_pct']}% | {bv('traceable_pct')} | {c['traceable_pct']}% |

Trace check = every trace line cites a `[clause]` that exists in the rule text AND the last line equals the reported tax.
Note (b)'s output size (700 tokens) is an assumption when not run. (c) is ~4x slower than (a) in-process because it interprets an AST, still <1 ms.

## 2. Plain-language input (Task 3)
Rule parser: {p['pass']}/{p['n']} gold texts correct; {p['ask_correct']}/{p['ask_cases']} ambiguous/incomplete/contradictory texts raised the right
clarifying question; **{p['guessed_when_should_ask']}** guessed values where a question was required. Caveat: {p['note']}
`parser.llm_parse` (Gemini extraction + the same validator) is implemented but NOT RUN here. Policy: the LLM may *extract*, never *fill gaps*.

## 3. The law changes (Task 5: FY 2025-26)
| | (a) Hand rules | (b) LLM query-time | (c) Compiled rules |
|---|---|---|---|
| Work needed | new `rules/fy2025_26.json` (keys changed: {', '.join(ch['config_keys_changed'])}); **0 engine lines** | new `rule_text/fy2025_26.md` only | LLM re-compile of the new rule text + human review + diff (`compile_rules.py`); stand-in JSON in repo |
| Time taken | [FILL: your stopwatch minutes] | [FILL] | [FILL] |
| What broke | nothing loud - engine was already data-driven | cannot be known without a run | nothing loud |
| Silent failure if FY not updated | **{a['stale_rules']['silently_wrong_with_stale_rules']}/{a['stale_rules']['fy25_26_cases']}** FY25-26 cases wrong with NO error | {NR} | **{c['stale_rules']['silently_wrong_with_stale_rules']}/{c['stale_rules']['fy25_26_cases']}** wrong, NO error |
| Unsupported FY | {a['unsupported_fy']} | n/a | {c['unsupported_fy']} |

Honest reading: because (a) and (c) were designed with the tax law in data, FY25-26 was a config change, not a code change - **I did not get a "what broke"
story from a genuinely structural change**, and I will not invent one. The measured silent failure is different and real: routing an FY25-26 taxpayer through
FY24-25 rules runs fine and is wrong {a['stale_rules']['silently_wrong_with_stale_rules']} times out of {a['stale_rules']['fy25_26_cases']} (new-regime users). Guard: regression tests pinned per FY
+ a release gate that fails if any FY lacks a golden suite. Structural changes (a new deduction, new surcharge rule) would need an engine change in (a), a new DSL function
in (c), and are the case where (b) is most dangerous: it will silently keep applying the old rule text it was not told changed.

## 4. Final design for 100,000 users (Task 6)
| Component | Type | Why (measured) |
|---|---|---|
| Free-text -> structured input | **Hybrid**: rule parser first; LLM extractor only when the rule parser cannot find salary/age; both feed one validator | Rules: {p['pass']}/{p['n']} on dev texts, free, deterministic, but brittle on unseen phrasing. LLM adds coverage; validator guarantees it cannot fill gaps |
| Clarifying questions | **Rule** | Missing/ambiguous/contradictory logic is a finite checklist; {p['ask_correct']}/{p['ask_cases']} correct, 0 guesses |
| Tax computation, both regimes | **Rule** (versioned config + small engine), (c) as the Budget-day accelerator | {a['exact_pct']}% exact, {a['deterministic_pct_10x']}% deterministic, ~{a['latency_ms_p50']} ms, ~$0 per 1,000. Tax must be rupee-exact and reproducible for audit |
| Regime recommendation + explanation text | **Rule** for the numbers; LLM may only *verbalise* the engine's trace | Every rupee has a clause (100%); an LLM narrating the trace cannot change a number if numbers are inserted by template |
| Budget-day rule updates | **LLM-assisted, human-gated**: LLM drafts config/DSL from the new text, tests + differential run against oracle decide | (c) makes the LLM a one-time author, not a runtime dependency |

Keeping it correct through the next Budget: (1) per-FY golden suites (boundaries around every new threshold) generated before code; (2) two independent implementations
(config engine vs compiled DSL) + oracle must agree on 100% of cases or deploy is blocked; (3) pin `fy` explicitly in every request and fail loudly on unknown FY;
(4) verify >=30 cases per FY on the official calculator (template provided); (5) canary: compare production outputs against the oracle nightly on sampled inputs;
(6) never use (b) for the number path - its cost/latency/non-determinism are unacceptable at 100k users and it cannot prove the stale-rule case above is fixed.

## 5. Known limits / things I did not verify
- Rule text and clause labels are my summary of the law; verify against incometax.gov.in/Finance Acts (no internet here). Rounding (Sec 288A/B to nearest Rs 10) follows my reading and is the most likely place to differ from the official calculator by Rs 1-9.
- Scope: salary only; 80CCD(2), professional tax, preventive health check-up, Sec 80TTA etc. excluded as per assignment.
- Oracle shares the author's reading of the law; it catches implementation bugs, not misreadings. Hence the official-calculator check.
- `compiled/*.json` in this repo is a **stand-in written by Claude**, not output of Gemini; regenerate with `compile_rules.py` and diff.
"""
(R.parent / "RESULTS_A.md").write_text(md)
print("RESULTS_A.md written")
